## Refactored with `async/await`

```js
// ─── Single fetch ───────────────────────────────────────────────
async function load(url) {
  const response = await fetch(url);

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}: ${response.statusText} (${url})`);
  }

  return response.json();
}

// ─── Parallel fetch (ordered results) ──────────────────────────
async function all(urls) {
  try {
    const results = await Promise.all(urls.map(load));
    return results;
  } catch (err) {
    throw new Error(`Batch load failed: ${err.message}`, { cause: err });
  }
}
```

### Usage

```js
// Single URL
try {
  const data = await load("https://api.example.com/items");
  console.log(data);
} catch (err) {
  console.error("Load failed:", err.message);
}

// Multiple URLs (parallel, ordered)
try {
  const [a, b, c] = await all([
    "https://api.example.com/a",
    "https://api.example.com/b",
    "https://api.example.com/c",
  ]);
  console.log(a, b, c);
} catch (err) {
  console.error("Batch failed:", err.message);
}
```

---

### What changed and why

| Original | Refactored | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `cb(null, j)` / `cb(e)` | `return` / `throw` | Eliminates callback-hell; errors propagate naturally |
| Manual counter `n` + `forEach` | `Promise.all(urls.map(load))` | Built-in parallelism with **ordered**
